 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 51,
   "text": "MERTHYR TYDFIL. Croeso! The iron capital of the world, once upon a time. East: Blaenafon, over the hill. South: the Rhondda and Caerdydd."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "BLAENAFON (Blaenavon). World Heritage, big pit, bigger weather. Mind the sheep on the road."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "merthyr_name",
   "title": "Merthyr Tydfil",
   "text": "Merthyr means 'martyr'. The town is named for Tydfil, a daughter of the Welsh king Brychan, who was killed by raiders about 480 AD and is remembered as a saint. There has been a church on the site since long before there was an ironworks."
  },
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "merthyr_iron",
   "title": "Iron Capital",
   "text": "In 1831 Merthyr was the largest town in Wales, with some 22,000 people, because of its four great ironworks: Cyfarthfa, Dowlais, Penydarren and Plymouth. They made rails, cannon and girders from iron ore, coal and limestone all found in the same hillsides."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 12,
   "local": {"cy": "Castell Cyfarthfa"},
   "plaque": "merthyr_cyfarthfa",
   "title": "Cyfarthfa Castle",
   "text": "William Crawshay II built Cyfarthfa Castle in 1824 as a home above his ironworks, with the furnaces in plain view of the dining room. His workers could see the castle from the works, and he could see them. It became a museum and art gallery in 1910."
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "merthyr_trevithick",
   "title": "21 February 1804",
   "text": "Here began the first railway journey by a steam locomotive. Richard Trevithick's engine hauled ten tons of iron, five wagons and about seventy men from Penydarren to Abercynon, nearly ten miles in around four hours. It won its owner a bet of 500 guineas, then broke the cast-iron rails it ran on."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "merthyr_rising",
   "title": "The Merthyr Rising",
   "text": "In June 1831 thousands of ironworkers, angry at wage cuts and unpayable debts, marched through Merthyr under a red flag, which is said to be the first time it was raised in Britain by workers in revolt. Soldiers fired on the crowd, and about two dozen protesters died."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "merthyr_dic",
   "title": "Dic Penderyn",
   "text": "Richard Lewis, known as Dic Penderyn, was hanged in Cardiff on 13 August 1831 for wounding a soldier during the Rising. Many thought him innocent. His last words are said to have been 'O Arglwydd, dyma gamwedd': 'O Lord, here is injustice'."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "merthyr_hardie",
   "title": "Keir Hardie",
   "text": "In 1900 Merthyr Tydfil elected James Keir Hardie, a Scot who had first gone down a mine aged ten, as one of its two MPs. He became the first leader of the Labour Party, and Merthyr kept sending him back until his death in 1915."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "merthyr_parry",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Joseph Parry",
   "text": "Joseph Parry was born in Merthyr in 1841 and worked in the ironworks as a boy. He emigrated to America and later returned to Wales. He wrote 'Myfanwy' in 1875, the love song every Welsh choir has on the bottom of its folder."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "merthyr_ynysfach",
   "title": "Ynysfach",
   "text": "The Ynysfach Engine House, built in 1836, housed the great blowing engine that forced air into the furnaces of Crawshay's second ironworks. It is now the Iron Heritage Centre, and it's the last building standing of the works."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "merthyr_market",
   "title": "Merthyr Market",
   "text": "Welsh markets are for cawl, Welsh cakes, secondhand books and the best conversation in town. In Merthyr Market you can buy a pair of wellies, a lamp, and a ten-minute opinion on the rugby."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "merthyr_taffvale",
   "title": "The Taff Vale",
   "text": "The Taff Vale Railway opened between Merthyr and Cardiff in 1841, carrying iron and coal to the docks. It later carried the people, and the Merthyr line is still a main way into the valleys."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "Trevithick's locomotive of 1804, with a stretch of the plate-way it ran on. It was heavy and the rails were brittle. A very important failure."
  }
 ],
